  <h1 class="article-title">Frame by Frame in Alight Motion: A Deeper Look</h1>
  <p class="article-meta">By <a href="/">WACD.ART</a> · August 2026</p>
  <div class="article-divider"></div>

  <p>This is a follow-up to the previous post about how I use Alight Motion. That one got more attention than I expected — so let me go deeper into the part that people seem to want to understand most: frame by frame animation.</p>

  <h2>Mouths from Sketchbook — frame by frame lip sync</h2>

  <p>When a character needs to speak, I draw all the mouth shapes separately in <strong>Sketchbook</strong> — different positions for different sounds. Open, half-open, closed, round, wide. I export each one as a PNG.</p>
  <p>Then in Alight Motion I place them on a layer, one by one, frame by frame — timed to match the voiceover. It is slow. But the result is something no automatic lip sync tool can give you: it feels <strong>alive</strong>.</p>
  <p>The key is to not try to match every single sound perfectly. Match the rhythm. Match the emotion. The eyes and body do the rest.</p>

  <h2>Vector points — frame by frame the hard way</h2>

  <p>For vector shapes I use the vector drawing tool inside Alight Motion. I set anchor points, then on the next keyframe I move those points to a new position. This gives smooth morphing between shapes.</p>
  <p>It looks complicated. The first time I tried it, I deleted everything three times. But once you understand that <strong>each keyframe is just a new position for the same points</strong> — it becomes logical. Slow, but logical.</p>

  <h2>The layer eye — my most important tool</h2>

  <p>The small eye icon on the left side of each layer in Alight Motion is the feature I use more than anything else. I keep 4, 5, sometimes 10 versions of the same element — different mouths, different hand positions, different expressions — all hidden.</p>
  <p>When I need one, I show it. When I don't, I hide it. <strong>Nothing gets deleted. Everything waits.</strong></p>

  <div class="quote-block">
    <p>In my creative process, I don't think too much — I try. Whatever I like more, I keep. The hidden layers are my safety net and my laboratory at the same time.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Why collage gives more freedom than drawing everything</h2>

  <p>In traditional 2D animation, every frame is drawn from scratch. In collage animation, I build a library of pieces. A fist. An open palm. A closed eye. An open eye. Eyebrows up. Eyebrows down. Dozens of cut-out elements, ready to be combined.</p>
  <p>This is not laziness. This is a different philosophy. <strong>The combinations are infinite. The result is always unique.</strong></p>
  <p>It reminds me of Louis de Funès in <em>L'homme orchestre</em> — humming musical ideas to his musicians, trusting them to catch his wave. I don't direct every pixel. I set the pieces in motion and let them find the story.</p>

  <h2>The honest answer to "can you do frame by frame in Alight Motion?"</h2>

  <p>Yes. But it is not the same as dedicated frame by frame apps like FlipaClip or Rough Animator. Alight Motion is a compositing and motion tool that <em>can</em> do frame by frame — if you understand its logic.</p>
  <p>The advantage: you can mix frame by frame with everything else in the same project. Collage elements, vector shapes, effects, text — all in one timeline. That combination is what makes my work look the way it does.</p>

  <p class="author">— WACD.ART · August 2026</p>

